      "id": "s2",
      "title": "Curious Kids: how do the clouds stay up in the sky?",
      "publisher": "The Conversation Media Group Ltd",
      "author": "McQuaid, Jim",
      "published_at": "2018-07-19",
      "url": "https://theconversation.com/curious-kids-how-do-the-clouds-stay-up-in-the-sky-99964",
      "retrieved_at": "2026-10-18T15:35:22.610Z",
      "sha256": "ecdd05512fa890870ddc7b5a897b8fb056e96119edffca79f92386a4474a6243",
      "rendered": false,
      "scraped": true,
      "excerpts": [
        "Skip to content Academic rigor, journalistic flair windy_sydney/Flickr , CC BY-NC Curious Kids: how do the clouds stay up in the sky?",
        "The Conversation is asking young people to send in questions they’d like an expert to answer.",
        "Believe it or not, I once weighed a cloud and not many people can say they have done that!",
        "My scientist friends and I flew up into the sky in a giant airship, and went all the way through a fluffy, white cloud.",
        "Actually, it was very wet up there, because clouds are made up of billions of tiny water droplets.",
        "As we flew through the cloud, we used lasers and other special scientific devices to measure how big the cloud was, and count how many tiny droplets of water were in it.",
        "Then, we did some maths and found that this cloud – which was actually pretty small, for a cloud – weighed four tonnes.",
        "So, you’re right to wonder how such a heavy thing can stay up in the sky.",
        "There are three pieces to this puzzle, and the first one is gravity.",
        "Like everything on this planet, the tiny droplets that make up a cloud are drawn towards the Earth by gravity.",
        "But these droplets are so small that it’s hard for them to push past all the air beneath them.",
        "This means that they don’t fall very fast at all – in fact, only about one centimetre per second ."
      ]
